# Part 1: Data Wrangling

### 1. Download the data file `hdd0318cy.sas7bdat`.

### 2. Use `read_sas` in library `haven` to read the data.

```{r}
# Import Data
library(haven)

df <- read_sas("C:\\Users\\student\\Downloads\\hdd0318cy.sas7bdat")
head(df)
```

### 3. Filter the data to have only patients of the year 2018 (yod=18)

### 4. Select to work with only following variables

```{r}
# 3 and 4
library(tidyverse)
df <- df |> filter(yod == 18) |> select("yod", "payfix","pay_ub92","age",  
                      "sex","raceethn","provider","moa", 
                      "yoa","mod","admtype", "asource" , 
                      "preopday" ,"los", "service" , "icu","ccu",    
                      "dispub92", "payer"  ,"drg","trandb", 
                      "randbg","randbs","orr", "anes","seq",   
                      "lab","dtest", "ther","blood","phar", 
                      "other","patcon","bwght","total","tot" ,  
                      "ecodub92","b_wt","pt_state","diag_adm","ancilar" ,
                      "campus","er_fee","er_chrg","er_mode","obs_chrg",
                      "obs_hour","psycchrg","nicu_day")
head(df)
```

```{r}
# Check Missings
colSums(is.na(df))
```


```{r}
# Write to CSV
write_csv(df, "midterm.csv")
```

### 5. What are variables that have missing values?

```{r}
library(tidyverse)
df <- read_csv("midterm.csv")
head(df)
```

```{r}
# Check Missings of CSV against SAS file
colSums(is.na(df))
```

Answer: `payfix`, `preopday`, `obs_hour`, and `nicu_day` have missing values according to the SAS dataset (a few lines above this). `raceethn`, `admtype`, `asource`, `bwght`, `ecodub92`, `pt_state`, `diag_adm`, `er_mode` are missing according to the CSV file, but I am deciding to keep in for the current moment because some of those variables are used for the model later and I would rather drop NAs from that column than drop the column as a whole.

### 6. Remove all variables with missing values.

```{r}
df <- df |> select(-c("payfix", "preopday", "obs_hour", "nicu_day"))
```

```{r}
# Verify
colSums(is.na(df))
```
(The columns I wanted to dropped were successfully dropped.)


```{r}
# Save cleaned file to CSV
write_csv(df, "midterm_cleaned.csv")
```

### 7. Refer to the data description in the file `HDD2015-18cy6-20-19.docx`, which variable recording the month of admission?, which variable recording the month of discharge?

Answer: `moa` is the month of admission variable, and `mod` is the month of discharge variable.

### 8. Which month admitted the most number of patients? Which month admitted the most number of male patients?

```{r}
df |> 
  count(moa) |> 
  arrange(-n)
```

```{r}
df |> 
  filter(sex == 1) |> 
  count(moa) |> 
  arrange(-n)
```

Answer: October admitted the most patients overall as well as male patients only.

### 9. Which month has the most number of teenage female patients?

```{r}
df |> 
  filter(between(age, 13,19)) |> 
  count(moa) |> 
  arrange(-n)
```

Answer: October also has the most teenage female admissions.

### 10. Which provider has the most number of female patients in October?

```{r}
df |> 
  filter(sex == 2 & moa == 10) |> 
  count(provider) |> 
  arrange(-n)
```

Answer: Rhode Island Hospital (7205) has the most female patients in October.

### 11. Are female patients older than male patients, on average?

```{r}
df <- df |> filter(sex != 9)
df |> count(sex)
```


```{r}
df |> 
  group_by(sex) |> 
  summarise(average_age =  mean(age)) |> 
  arrange(-average_age)
```

Answer: Male patients are older than female patients on average.

### 12. Calculate the average age of patients by months. Which month has the oldest patients on average age?

```{r}
df |> 
  group_by(moa) |> 
  summarise(average_age = mean(age)) |> 
  arrange(-average_age)
```

Answer: January has the oldest patients on average.

### 13. What is the name of the provider that has the highest total charge?

```{r}
df |> 
  group_by(provider) |> 
  summarise(average_total_charge = mean(tot)) |> 
  arrange(-average_total_charge)
```

Answer: Bradley (7215) has the hightest average total charge.

### 14. What is the name of the provider that has the least total charge for teenage male on average?

```{r}
df |> 
  filter(sex == 1 & between(age, 13, 19)) |> 
  group_by(provider) |> 
  summarise(average_total_charge = mean(tot)) |> 
  arrange(average_total_charge)
```

Answer: Roger Williams (7206) has the lowest average total charge for teenage male patients.

### 15. Create a season (Spring, Summer, Fall, Winter) variable. Calculate the length of stays by season. Which season has the longest length of stays on average?

```{r}
# Create season variable

df$season <- case_when(df$moa %in% c(12, 1, 2) ~ "Winter",
                       df$moa %in% c(3, 4, 5) ~ "Spring",
                       df$moa %in% c(6, 7, 8) ~ "Summer",
                       df$moa %in% c(9, 10, 11) ~ "Fall")

head(df)
```

```{r}
df |> 
  group_by(season) |> 
  summarise(avg_length_of_stays = mean(los)) |> 
  arrange(-avg_length_of_stays)
```

Answer: Summer has the highest average length of stays.

### 16. On average, how much a 20 year-old male get charged for staying 1 day in the Fall season?

```{r}
df |> 
  filter(age == 20 & sex == 1 & los == 1 & season == "Fall") |> 
  group_by(season) |> 
  summarise(avg_charge = mean(tot))
```
  

Answer: On average, a 20-year old male gets charged $16,511 for staying 1 day in the fall season.

### 17. Write a paragraph to summarize the section and give your comments on the results. You could do some other calculations to support your points.

There are many findings from the hospitalization dataset found from this data wrangling. For example, we got a better idea of the scope of the dataset. For example, we learned that there are more females patients in the dataset than males, but that males are of higher age than females. Additionally, October seems to be the most frequent month for being admitted, because it is the month with the most patients admitted overall as well as by male only. The average total cost of a hospital visit at a provider is at most $69,945.55 at Bradley, but the most common provider is not Bradley but rather Rhode Island Hospital (see below code chunk). Based on the seasonality analysis, summer and fall are roughly the same in terms of average length of a stay, however there is not much variation by season, demonstrating that season might not influence the length of stay for a patient as much as another variable might. There is still much more analysis that could be done, which will be explored in the Data Visualization section of the project.

```{r}
# Most COmmon Provider Overall
df |> 
  count(provider) |> 
  arrange(-n)
```


# Part 2: Data Visualization

### Plot 1: Age Distribution
```{r}
plot1 <- df |> ggplot() + 
                geom_histogram(mapping = aes(x = age)) +
                labs(x = 'Age',
                     y = 'Count',
                     title = 'Age Distribution',
                     caption = 'Age is bimodally distributed with a peak with age 0 and with older adults.')
ggsave(filename = 'midterm_plot1.png', plot = plot1)
```

### Plot 2: Sex Distribution

```{r}
df$sex_name <- case_when(df$sex ==  1 ~ "Male",
                         df$sex ==  2 ~ "Female")
```


```{r}
plot2 <- df |> ggplot() + 
          geom_bar(mapping = aes(x = sex_name, fill = sex_name)) + 
          scale_fill_manual(values = c("Male" = "lightblue", "Female" = "pink")) +
          theme(legend.position = "none") + 
          labs(x = 'Sex',
               y = 'Count',
               title = 'Sex Distribution',
               caption = 'There are more female patients in the dataset than male patients.')
ggsave(filename = 'midterm_plot2.png', plot = plot2)
```

### Plot 3: Cost Distribution

```{r}
plot3 <- df |> ggplot() + 
               geom_histogram(mapping = aes(x = tot), bins = 50) +
               labs(x = 'Cost',
               y = 'Amount',
               title = 'Cost Distribution',
               caption = 'The total cost is heavily skewed right, with extreme outliers in the expensive tail.')
ggsave(filename = 'midterm_plot3.png', plot = plot3)
```


### Plot 4: Cost by Gender

```{r}
plot4 <- df |> ggplot() + 
               geom_boxplot(mapping = aes(x = tot, y = sex_name)) +
               labs(x = 'Cost',
               y = 'Sex',
               title = 'Cost Distribution by Gender',
               caption = 'The total cost for both male and female has extreme outliers, but females have the most extreme outlier in the dataset.')
ggsave(filename = 'midterm_plot4.png', plot = plot4)
```


### Plot 5: Patient Age versus Total Cost

```{r}
plot5 <- df |> 
          slice_sample(n=1000) |> 
          ggplot(aes(x = age, y = tot)) + 
          geom_point() + 
          geom_smooth(method = "lm", se = FALSE, color = "blue") +
          labs(x = 'Age',
               y = 'Total Cost',
               title = 'Age vs. Cost (n = 1,000)',
               caption = 'There appears to be a slight trend of cost increasing with patient age.')
ggsave(filename = 'midterm_plot5.png', plot = plot5)
```

### Plot 6: Cost by Race/Ethnicity

```{r}
# Map race numbers to names
df$race_name <- case_when(df$raceethn == 1 ~ "White",
                          df$raceethn == 2 ~ "Black",
                          df$raceethn == 3 ~ "Asian",
                          df$raceethn == 4 ~ "American Indian",
                          df$raceethn == 5 ~ "Native Hawaiian",
                          df$raceethn == 6 ~ "Other",
                          df$raceethn == 7 ~ "Hispanic",
                          df$raceethn == 9 ~ "Unknown")

```


```{r}
plot6 <- df |> 
            filter(!is.na(race_name)) |> 
            ggplot(aes(x = race_name, y = tot, group = race_name)) + 
            geom_boxplot() +
            theme(legend.position = "none") +
            labs(x = 'Race / Ethnicity',
                 y = 'Cost',
                 title = 'Cost by Race/Ethnicity',
                 caption = 'There are variations in the cost distribution by race.')
ggsave(filename = 'midterm_plot6.png', plot = plot6)
```


### Plot 7: Length of Stay Distribution

```{r}
plot7 <- df |> ggplot() + 
               geom_histogram(mapping = aes(x = los), bins = 50) +
               labs(x = 'Length of Stay',
               y = 'Days',
               title = 'Length of Stay Distribution',
               caption = 'Patient length of stay is heavily skewed right, with a max stay of 528 days .')
ggsave(filename = 'midterm_plot7.png', plot = plot7)
```
```{r}
summary(df$los)
```

### Plot 8:  Monthly Admissions by Ethnicity

```{r}
plot8 <- df |> 
          filter(!is.na(race_name) & race_name!="White") |>
          count(moa, race_name) |>
          ggplot(aes(x = moa, y = n, fill = race_name)) +
          geom_col(position = "fill") +
          labs(x = "Month of Admission",
               y = "Percentage of Admissions",
               fill = "Race/Ethnicity",
               title = "Monthly Admissions by Ethnicity (Excluding White)",
               caption = "Hispanic and Black patients are the next most common races/ethnicities to be admitted after White patients.")
ggsave(filename = 'midterm_plot8.png', plot = plot8)
```


### Plot 9: Average Monthly Cost

```{r}
plot9 <-  df |> 
            group_by(moa) |> 
            summarise(avg_cost = mean(tot, na.rm = TRUE)) |> 
            ggplot(aes(x = moa, y = avg_cost)) +
            geom_line() +
            labs(x = "Month of Admission",
                 y = "Average Cost",
                 title = "Average Cost by Month",
                 caption = "There are drastic fluctuations in the average cost over time.")
ggsave(filename = 'midterm_plot9.png', plot = plot9)
```





### Plot 10: Average Monthly Cost by Provider

```{r}
df$provider <- as.factor(df$provider)
```


```{r}
plot10 <- df |> 
            group_by(moa, provider) |> 
            summarise(avg_cost = mean(tot, na.rm = TRUE)) |> 
            ggplot(aes(x = moa, y = avg_cost, color = provider)) +
            geom_line() +
            labs(x = "Month of Admission",
                 y = "Average Cost",
                 color = "Provider Code",
                 title = "Average Cost by Month by Provider",
                 caption = "Most providers have similar average monthly costs, but Bradley consistently has the highest average cost.")
ggsave(filename = 'midterm_plot10.png', plot = plot10)
```
```{r}
df |> group_by(moa, provider) |> summarise(avg_cost = mean(tot, na.rm = TRUE)) |> arrange(-avg_cost)
```


### Animated Plot: Admissions by Month by Sex

```{r}
library(gganimate)

animated <- df |> 
  count(moa, sex_name) |>
  ggplot(aes(x = moa, y = n, color = sex_name)) +
  geom_line() +
  geom_point() +
  transition_reveal(moa) +
  labs(x = 'Month of Admittance',
       y = 'Count',
       color = "Sex",
       title = 'Admissions by Month by Sex',
       caption = 'There is a similar trend of admissions by month for males and females.')
anim_save('midterm_animated.gif', animation = animated)
```

### Write a paragraph to summarize the section and give your comments on the results.
From this exercise, I learned that a lot of data storytelling is done through visualizations. It is important to understand the data you are working with by getting statistics on the demographics of the patients. From that, I learned that there are more females than males in the dataset and that the age is bimodally distributed. I also learned that the cost and length of stays are heavily skewed right. Once basic charts are complete, you can get into more complex ideas. I focused on cost because I wanted to see if one gender or race was targeted cost-wise, but based on the analyses, there is no evidence to support that idea. I also wanted to look at the monthly trends, and saw that while there are large fluctuations in the average monthly cost, there aren't huge differences in cost between providers. With the wide array of variables to choose from, this analysis could be taken much further, but I wanted to narrow in on cost trends.

# Part 3: Predictive Models

### 1. Use `filter` function to filter out rows where `raceethn==''` or `admtype==''`. Make sure all the categorical variables are factor, numeric variables are numeric. Set Training : Testing Split = 10 : 90
```{r}
# Filter
df <- df |> filter(raceethn != '' & admtype != '')
```

```{r}
str(df)
```

```{r}
summary(df$tot)
```

```{r}
# Create target
median <- 22124
df$target <- case_when(df$tot < median ~ "Low",
                       df$tot >= median ~ "High")
```


```{r}
# Set character datatypes to factors
df <- df |> mutate(across(where(is.character), as.factor))
str(df)
```

```{r}
# Select only certain variables
df_model <- df |> select(c("age","sex","raceethn","provider","moa","mod","admtype","campus","los","target"))
```


```{r}
# Train/Test Split
library(caret)
set.seed(2020)
splitIndex <- createDataPartition(df_model$target, p = .90, 
                                  list = FALSE)
df_train <- df_model[ splitIndex,]
df_test <- df_model[-splitIndex,]
```


### 2. Train a decision tree using `rpart`. Plot the decision tree. Plot the variable importance ranked by the tree.

```{r}
# Train Model
library(rpart)
tree_model <- rpart(target ~ ., data = df_train)
```

```{r}
# Plot Tree
library(rattle)
fancyRpartPlot(tree_model)
```

```{r}
# Plot Variable Importance
barplot(tree_model$variable.importance)

```

### 3. Using caret for this question. Set `Training Control` to be: Use Cross-Validation of 5 folds across all models. Train & tune at least 2 different models (i.e. two different values for `method=` in the train function of caret). Plot the hyper-parameter tuning plots for each model.

```{r}
library(caret)

training_control <- trainControl(method = "cv", number = 5)
df_train_sample <- df_train |> slice_sample(n = 15000)
```


```{r}
str(df)
```


```{r}
# Model 1: Random Forest
model_rf <- train(target ~.,
                  data = df_train_sample,
                  method = "rf",
                  trControl = training_control,
                  tuneLength = 3)
```

```{r}
# Model 1 Variable Importance
plot(model_rf)
```


```{r}
# Model 2: KNN
model_knn <- train(target ~.,
                   data = df_train_sample,
                   method = "knn",
                   trControl = training_control,
                   tuneLength = 5)
```

```{r}
# Model 2 Variable Importance
plot(model_knn)
```


### 4. Plot the comparison of the models in 3.

```{r}
results <- resamples(list("Random Forest" = model_rf,
                          "KNN" = model_knn))

png(filename = "rf_knn_comparison.png", width = 800, height = 600)
bwplot(results)
dev.off()
```


### 5. What is your final selection for the model? Test the accuracy of your final model on the test data.

```{r}
pred <- predict(model_rf, df_test)

cm <- confusionMatrix(data = pred, reference = df_test$target, positive = "High")

cm$overall[1]
```


### 6. Create another `target` variable (binary), decide the input variables and redo 1 to 5.

```{r}
df_model_2 <- df_model
```

```{r}
df_model_2 <- df_model_2 |> select(-target)
df_model_2 <- df_model_2 |> rename(target = moa) 

df_model_2$target <- case_when(df_model_2$target %in% c(1:6) ~ "Early_Year",
                               df_model_2$target %in% c(7:12) ~ "Late_Year")


df_model_2
```

```{r}
# Set character datatypes to factors
df_model_2 <- df_model_2 |> mutate(across(where(is.character), as.factor))
str(df_model_2)
```

```{r}
# Train/Test Split
library(caret)
set.seed(2020)
splitIndex <- createDataPartition(df_model_2$target, p = .90, 
                                  list = FALSE)
df_train <- df_model_2[ splitIndex,]
df_test <- df_model_2[-splitIndex,]
```


```{r}
# Train Model
library(rpart)
tree_model <- rpart(target ~ ., data = df_train)
```

```{r}
# Plot Tree
library(rattle)
fancyRpartPlot(tree_model)
```

```{r}
# Plot Variable Importance
barplot(tree_model$variable.importance)

```

```{r}
# Choose 2 Models
library(caret)

training_control <- trainControl(method = "cv", number = 5)
df_train_sample <- df_train |> slice_sample(n = 15000)
```



```{r}
# Model 1: Random Forest
model_rf <- train(target ~.,
                  data = df_train_sample,
                  method = "rf",
                  trControl = training_control,
                  tuneLength = 3)
```

```{r}
# Model 1 Variable Importance
plot(model_rf)
```


```{r}
# Model 2: KNN
model_knn <- train(target ~.,
                   data = df_train_sample,
                   method = "knn",
                   trControl = training_control,
                   tuneLength = 5)
```

```{r}
# Model 2 Variable Importance
plot(model_knn)
```


```{r}
# Model Comparison
results <- resamples(list("Random Forest" = model_rf,
                          "KNN" = model_knn))

png(filename = "rf_knn_comparison_2.png", width = 800, height = 600)
bwplot(results)
dev.off()
```

```{r}
# Final selection: Random Forest
pred <- predict(model_rf, df_test)

cm <- confusionMatrix(data = pred, reference = df_test$target, positive = "Early_Year")

cm$overall[1]
```

### Write a paragraph to summarize the section and give your comments on the results.
Modeling on cost was very interesting, especially when we compare the decision tree to the random forest and KNN models. The decision tree is a great basic model that is easy to interpret, but lacks at finding the true patterns in the data. However, its simplicity and easy-to-follow diagram makes it easy to conceptualize. Comparing the random forest to KNN is interesting because a random forest is an eager learner whereas KNN is a lazy learner. The random forest performed better, likely because of the "eager learning" where it spends much time training on the data to make more accurate predictions in the testing phase. I then chose to try and predict the Month of Admittance for the second phase of modeling, but I kept the same input variables as before. While this gave me great results in both the random forest and KNN models, I need to make a more educated decision on what variables to model on because I think the MOD is too highly correlated with MOA. However, I do believe this experience was needed to teach me the importance of variable selection in the modeling process and I intentionally left it in the project to bring up such point. Overall, the random forest model is unsurprisingly the better predictor, but if I used gradient boosting or ada boosting, it would be a much more interesting (but time consuming) comparison.
